\section*{Equivalence of Nuclear Dimension, Z-Stability, and Strict Comparison}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Manuscript 1 (family 291, 2026-09-23): A separable nuclear C*-algebra that is simple and non-elementary is claimed to absorb Jiang--Su under extended-functional strict comparison, including nonunital and unbounded-trace cases; semigroup divisibility also yields a nonsimple criterion. Manuscript 2 (family 291, 2026-09-23): Finite nuclear dimension is claimed to characterize Jiang--Su stability for separable nuclear algebras lacking elementary ideal subquotients; moreover, Jiang--Su stabilization always lowers nuclear dimension to at most one. Manuscript 3 (family 302, 2026-09-25): Whenever an infinite compact metrizable system is minimal under a homeomorphism, its crossed-product comparison radius is claimed to be half its mean dimension, including infinite values; zero forces nuclear dimension at most one.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 291, 2026-09-23): Trace transfer into small hereditary supports followed by spectral rounding establishes full Cuntz-semigroup divisibility. Pure-state compression produces norm-visible matrix cones, while localized nuclear approximations produce trace-visible ones. Comparison packs their defects into a central corner, constructing dimension-drop maps whose lifted intertwining relations give Jiang--Su absorption.

Manuscript 2 (family 291, 2026-09-23): Localized order-zero approximations control ranks even when traces are infinite on some ideals. A supporting map and comparison yield two-color approximations that survive stabilization and hereditary passage. Conversely, Gaussian sign tensors and pure-state excision construct two full orthogonal central elements from the absence of characters, supplying the absorption criterion.

Manuscript 3 (family 302, 2026-09-25): Finite-support comparison witnesses are converted to bundle embeddings, which feed the companion's boundary-preserving compression theorem. Packet reconstruction translates that compression into a smaller cover without introducing absent colors. Applying the construction on aligned orbit blocks contradicts any larger cover-growth rate, providing the missing lower bound; established upper bounds and regularity results finish the equality and zero-dimensional consequences.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 291, 2026-09-23): Strict comparison implies Jiang–Su absorption in the full separable simple nuclear nonelementary setting; together with the standard already-proved Jiang–Su/nuclear-dimension equivalence and Jiang–Su implies strict comparison, this completes exactly this problem three-way equivalence.

Manuscript 2 (family 291, 2026-09-23): The governing theorem establishes the finite-nuclear-dimension/Jiang–Su equivalence, but this problem additionally asks that strict comparison imply those properties.

Manuscript 3 (family 302, 2026-09-25): The primary manuscript establishes radius of comparison equals half mean dimension for every minimal infinite compact-metrizable Z system, with zero-dimensionality regularity equivalences. The result proves the radius/mean-dimension identity and comparison/absorption/nuclear-dimension relations for minimal Z crossed products C(X) crossed\_h Z. It does not quantify over every simple nuclear algebra, nonunital algebra, or all decomposition-rank clauses of the selected statement.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 291, the scope page documents the following selections. The selection gives uniform Gamma from real rank zero of the designated tracial ultrapower. The documented comparison scope also treats Jiang--Su absorption and Gamma under strict comparison, with additional trace and target-rank conditions in the projectionless case. Manuscript 1 is not among the papers selected by that page, so the family link does not formalize its main conclusion. It does not establish formal coverage of the complete problem clause. Manuscript 2 is not among the papers selected by that page, so the family link does not formalize its main conclusion. It does not establish formal coverage of the complete problem clause.

Family 302 has no Lean scope page in this snapshot; no formalization of these manuscript results is listed.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

